            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Common Performance Issues</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>N+1 queries:</strong> Loading related data in loops</li>
                        <li><strong>Over-fetching:</strong> Loading all columns when you need a few</li>
                        <li><strong>Cartesian explosion:</strong> Multiple Includes causing data duplication</li>
                        <li><strong>Client evaluation:</strong> Processing data in memory instead of database</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Projection (Select Only What You Need)</h2>
                    <p>Project only the columns you need to avoid over-fetching. The left example loads entire entities then projects in memory (inefficient); prefer projecting in the query so SQL only returns required columns.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ❌ Bad - loads entire entity
var products = await context.Products.ToListAsync();
var names = products.Select(p => p.Name);

// ✅ Good - only loads Name column
var names = await context.Products
    .Select(p => p.Name)
    .ToListAsync();

// ✅ Project to DTO
var dtos = await context.Products
    .Select(p => new ProductDto { Id = p.Id, Name = p.Name })
    .ToListAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>No-Tracking for Read-Only</h2>
                    <p>No-tracking queries skip change tracking overhead. Use <code>AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution()</code> when you still need stable identity for included entities.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ✅ No tracking overhead for read-only queries
var products = await context.Products
    .AsNoTracking()
    .ToListAsync();

// ✅ No tracking with identity resolution
var products = await context.Products
    .AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution()
    .Include(p => p.Category)
    .ToListAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Compiled Queries</h2>
                    <p>Compile frequently-used queries to avoid expression tree re-processing and improve performance for repeated executions.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Pre-compile frequently used queries
private static readonly Func&lt;AppDbContext, int, Task&lt;Product?&gt;&gt; GetProductById =
    EF.CompileAsyncQuery((AppDbContext context, int id) =>
        context.Products.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Id == id));

// Usage (faster for repeated calls)
var product = await GetProductById(context, 42);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Raw SQL and Stored Procedures</h2>
                    <p>Use raw SQL for very complex queries or to reuse existing stored procedures; prefer parameterized or interpolated APIs provided by EF Core to avoid SQL injection.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Raw SQL for complex queries
var products = await context.Products
    .FromSqlRaw("SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Price > {0}", minPrice)
    .ToListAsync();

// Interpolated (SQL injection safe)
var products = await context.Products
    .FromSqlInterpolated($"SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Price > {minPrice}")
    .ToListAsync();

// Execute non-query
await context.Database
    .ExecuteSqlRawAsync("UPDATE Products SET Stock = 0 WHERE Discontinued = 1");</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Bulk Operations</h2>
                    <p>Avoid per-entity saves in loops—batch inserts/updates/delete with AddRange or the bulk APIs (EF Core 7+) to reduce round trips to the database.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ❌ Slow - individual inserts
foreach (var product in products)
{
    context.Products.Add(product);
    await context.SaveChangesAsync();  // N database calls!
}

// ✅ Better - batch insert
context.Products.AddRange(products);
await context.SaveChangesAsync();  // 1 database call

// ✅ Best for large sets - EF Core 7+ bulk operations
await context.Products
    .Where(p => p.CategoryId == oldCategoryId)
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(p => p.CategoryId, newCategoryId));

await context.Products
    .Where(p => p.IsDeleted)
    .ExecuteDeleteAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Logging Generated SQL</h2>
                    <p>Log generated SQL to analyze and optimize queries; <code>EnableSensitiveDataLogging()</code> shows parameter values but avoid enabling it in production.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// In Program.cs
options.UseSqlServer(connectionString)
    .LogTo(Console.WriteLine, LogLevel.Information)
    .EnableSensitiveDataLogging();  // Shows parameter values

// Or use a logger
options.UseLoggerFactory(LoggerFactory.Create(builder => builder
    .AddConsole()
    .SetMinimumLevel(LogLevel.Information)));</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use Select to load only needed columns.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>AsNoTracking for read-only scenarios.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>ExecuteUpdateAsync/ExecuteDeleteAsync for bulk ops.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Always check generated SQL for complex queries.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

